WEBVTT

00:00:01.000 --> 00:00:07.043
A site sends email for account confirmation, password reset, workflows, and notifications.

00:00:07.043 --> 00:00:12.708
The Azure Communication Services Email module delivers those messages through Azure.

00:00:12.708 --> 00:00:17.870
In this video, we enable it, configure its provider, and open the test page.

00:00:22.470 --> 00:00:27.731
The Email module sends the messages of your site through its default provider.

00:00:27.731 --> 00:00:33.305
A provider is the service that delivers a message, such as an SMTP server or Azure.

00:00:33.305 --> 00:00:39.130
Account emails, workflow Email Tasks, and email notifications use the default provider.

00:00:39.880 --> 00:00:44.225
The Azure Communication Services Email module adds two providers.

00:00:44.225 --> 00:00:48.019
Azure Communication Services is configured in the admin.

00:00:48.019 --> 00:00:53.771
Default Azure Communication Services is listed only when the server configuration sets a

00:00:53.771 --> 00:00:57.810
sender and a connection string, so the admin cannot edit it.

00:00:58.560 --> 00:01:02.979
To use the first provider, you need an Azure Communication Services resource, a

00:01:02.979 --> 00:01:06.670
sender email address, and the connection string of that resource.

00:01:10.970 --> 00:01:15.909
Let's open Tools, Features, and search for Azure Communication Services Email.

00:01:15.909 --> 00:01:19.320
The feature is listed in the Communication category.

00:01:19.793 --> 00:01:21.164
Let's select Enable.

00:01:21.164 --> 00:01:25.274
Orchard Core also enables the Email feature it depends on, and a message

00:01:25.274 --> 00:01:27.593
confirms that the feature was enabled.

00:01:31.893 --> 00:01:34.917
Let's open Settings, Communication, Email.

00:01:34.917 --> 00:01:39.452
The page has two tabs, Providers and Azure Communication Services.

00:01:39.452 --> 00:01:43.483
The Providers tab warns that no email provider is enabled.

00:01:43.935 --> 00:01:50.287
On the Azure Communication Services tab, the checkbox Enable Azure Provider turns the provider on.

00:01:50.287 --> 00:01:53.035
Let's select it, and two fields appear.

00:01:53.524 --> 00:01:57.037
Sender email address is the address the messages come from.

00:01:57.037 --> 00:02:01.307
Its hint says the address is used unless the message sets its own sender.

00:02:01.307 --> 00:02:03.144
Let's type a sender address.

00:02:03.617 --> 00:02:08.689
Connection String takes the connection string of your Azure Communication Services resource.

00:02:08.689 --> 00:02:10.552
The field hides what you type.

00:02:10.552 --> 00:02:12.157
Let's enter a fake value.

00:02:12.607 --> 00:02:14.206
Both fields are required.

00:02:14.206 --> 00:02:18.487
Let's select Save, and the page confirms that the site settings were updated.

00:02:18.946 --> 00:02:24.723
The Connection String field is empty again, and its placeholder says the value is securely stored.

00:02:24.723 --> 00:02:29.556
Leave it blank to keep the stored value, or type a different value to replace it.

00:02:30.061 --> 00:02:34.693
On the Providers tab, Default provider lists Azure Communication Services.

00:02:34.693 --> 00:02:39.441
When no default provider is set, enabling the provider makes it the default.

00:02:43.741 --> 00:02:46.407
Let's open Tools, Testing, Email Test.

00:02:46.407 --> 00:02:51.921
The Provider list shows the enabled providers, and it starts on the default provider.

00:02:52.419 --> 00:02:55.772
Provider, To, Subject, and Body are required.

00:02:55.772 --> 00:02:59.979
Let's enter a fake recipient, a subject, and a short body.

00:03:02.433 --> 00:03:05.909
Sender, Cc, Bcc, and Reply To are optional.

00:03:05.909 --> 00:03:12.223
When Sender is empty, the provider uses the sender email address from its settings.

00:03:12.690 --> 00:03:17.583
With a real resource, Send delivers the message through the selected provider, and

00:03:17.583 --> 00:03:20.807
the page confirms it with Message sent successfully.

00:03:20.807 --> 00:03:24.310
The values here are fake, so we leave the message unsent.

00:03:28.510 --> 00:03:32.981
If more than one provider is enabled, choose the default on the Providers tab.

00:03:32.981 --> 00:03:37.080
The Email module sends the messages of your site through that provider.

00:03:37.830 --> 00:03:42.158
Clearing Enable Azure Provider and saving turns the provider off.

00:03:42.158 --> 00:03:46.730
If this provider was the default, the default setting is cleared too.

00:03:47.480 --> 00:03:51.282
When the call to Azure fails, the Email Test page shows a general

00:03:51.282 --> 00:03:54.602
error message that never includes the connection string.

00:03:54.602 --> 00:03:57.440
The details are written to the log of the site.

00:03:58.190 --> 00:04:02.806
When the Secrets feature is enabled, the Connection String field can also reference

00:04:02.806 --> 00:04:05.970
a secret, instead of keeping the value in the settings.

00:04:07.970 --> 00:04:13.719
The Azure Communication Services Email module sends the email of your site through an Azure resource.

00:04:13.719 --> 00:04:18.179
Enable the feature, enter the sender and the connection string, and check the

00:04:18.179 --> 00:04:20.113
result on the Email Test page.

00:04:20.113 --> 00:04:21.510
Thanks for watching.

